---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<Layout
  title="Terms"
  description="Terms for supply and fit of made-to-measure worktops by Cheshire Stoneworks. Draft for review."
  noindex
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Legal</span>
      <h1>Terms of business</h1>
      <p class="notice notice--review">
        REVIEW: this is a draft skeleton, not legal advice. It sets out the headings a supply-and-fit
        contract for made-to-measure worktops normally needs, with notes on where the Consumer
        Contracts Regulations 2013 and the Consumer Rights Act 2015 bite. Have it reviewed before
        it is used, and make sure the written quote you send before taking a deposit carries the
        pre-contract information listed at the bottom.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Who you are dealing with</h2>
      <p>
        {site.legal.registeredName}, company number {site.legal.companyNumber}, registered office
        {site.legal.registeredOffice}. Contact {site.contact.email} or {site.contact.phoneDisplay}.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Quotes and the contract</h2>
      <p>
        Indicative prices given from a plan are estimates. A written quote follows the template
        visit and states the material, colour, thickness, edge profile, cut-outs, upstands or
        splashbacks, and the total price including VAT and fitting. The contract is formed when you
        accept the written quote and pay the deposit. [TODO: confirm process.]
      </p>

      <h2>3. Deposit and payment</h2>
      <p>
        A deposit of [TODO: percentage] is payable on acceptance and before material is ordered. The
        balance is due [TODO: on completion of the install / within N days]. [TODO: payment methods.]
      </p>

      <h2>4. Made to measure: cancellation</h2>
      <p>
        Worktops are cut to a template of your kitchen and cannot be resold. Under the Consumer
        Contracts Regulations 2013, the 14-day right to cancel does not apply to goods made to the
        consumer's specification or clearly personalised. [TODO: state clearly what is refundable if a
        customer cancels before the template, after the template but before cutting, and after
        cutting. Be specific and fair; an adviser should check that the deposit is not a penalty.]
      </p>

      <h2>5. Your kitchen must be ready</h2>
      <p>
        You are responsible for the kitchen being ready for template and for install as described on
        the <a href="/how-it-works">how it works page</a>. A wasted visit because the kitchen is not
        ready may be charged at [TODO: amount].
      </p>

      <h2>6. Natural materials</h2>
      <p>
        Granite and other natural stones vary in colour, veining and texture, and may contain fissures
        and pits. Samples and showroom slabs are indicative. Engineered quartz and ultra-compact
        surfaces may show slight variation between batches. Joins are filled with colour-matched
        resin and will be visible on close inspection.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Fitting</h2>
      <p>
        We fix, join and seal the worktops and fit upstands. We do not plumb in sinks or connect
        hobs. We are not responsible for units that are not level or fixed, or for walls that are out
        of square, beyond what a template can accommodate.
      </p>

      <h2>8. Guarantee and your statutory rights</h2>
      <p>
        {site.guarantee.summary}. {site.guarantee.remedy}. This is in addition to your rights under the
        Consumer Rights Act 2015, which require goods to be of satisfactory quality, fit for purpose
        and as described, and services to be carried out with reasonable care and skill.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Complaints</h2>
      <p>
        Contact us first at {site.contact.email}. [TODO: state your complaints process and any
        alternative dispute resolution scheme you belong to, or state that you do not.]
      </p>

      <h2>10. Pre-contract information checklist</h2>
      <p class="notice notice--review">
        REVIEW: for an off-premises or distance contract with a consumer, the Consumer Contracts
        Regulations 2013 (Schedule 2) require the following to be given in a durable medium before the
        consumer is bound, which in practice means on or with the written quote, before the deposit:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The main characteristics of the goods and services (the specification).</li>
        <li>Our identity, geographic address, phone and email.</li>
        <li>The total price including VAT, or how it will be calculated, plus any delivery or other charges.</li>
        <li>Arrangements for payment, delivery and performance, and the time by which we will perform.</li>
        <li>Our complaint handling policy.</li>
        <li>A statement that the right to cancel does not apply because the goods are made to the consumer's specification.</li>
        <li>The existence of the legal guarantee of conformity, and details of our own guarantee.</li>
        <li>Duration of the contract, and any deposit or financial guarantees we require.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted">
        Failure to give this information can extend the customer's cancellation rights and affect our
        ability to charge for work done. Check the quote template with an adviser.
      </p>

      <p class="muted">Last updated: [TODO: date].</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
